.TH SNATCHER 1 2026-05-30 snatcher "Snatcher Manual"
.SH NAME
snatcher \- Transmission BitTorrent CLI client
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B snatcher
.RI [ "global options" ]
.I command
.RI [ "command options" ]
.RI [ arguments ]
.br
.B snatcher
.RB [ \-\-version ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B snatcher
is a modern CLI for managing the Transmission BitTorrent client.
It is built so that anything the (forthcoming) TUI can do is also scriptable
from the command line.
.PP
snatcher requires
.B Transmission 4.0 or newer
(RPC version 17 or higher) and refuses to operate against older servers,
reporting an unsupported\-version error instead of failing in confusing ways.
.PP
Commands speak to a single configured Transmission server over its RPC
interface. The non-secret connection settings (server URL and username) are
stored in a JSON config file; the password is kept in a secret store, by
default the operating system keyring (see
.BR SECRETS ).
Run
.B snatcher server add
once to configure a server before using the other commands.
.PP
Every command that produces output renders in one of three modes with a fixed
precedence:
.BR \-\-jq " \(-> " \-\-json " \(-> plaintext."
The JSON field names are a stable scripting contract. Human and plaintext
output is sanitized of terminal control sequences (server-derived names,
labels, errors, and paths are untrusted); JSON and jq output is never
sanitized, since it is a structured contract the consumer handles.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Global options
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print version information and exit. Valid on
.B snatcher
itself.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show help for
.B snatcher
or any subcommand and exit.
.SS Common options
These options are accepted by the
.BR server ,
.BR torrent ,
and
.B config
subcommands. They do not all apply everywhere: the named-server registry
subcommands
.RB ( server\ add ", " server\ list ", " server\ default ", " server\ show ", " server\ ping ", " server\ remove )
read or write local config and pick a server by positional alias, so they take
.B \-\-config
(and
.BR \-\-json / \-\-jq
where they emit data) but never
.BR \-\-server .
The
.B config
subcommands never contact a server at all, so they take
.BR \-\-config ", " \-\-json ", and " \-\-jq
but never
.BR \-\-server .
The
.B api
command also accepts
.B \-\-config
and
.B \-\-jq
but uses
.B \-\-raw
in place of
.BR \-\-json .
.TP
.BR \-\-config " " \fIpath\fR
Read configuration from
.I path
instead of the default location (see
.BR FILES ).
.TP
.BR \-\-server " " \fIalias\fR
Target the configured server named
.IR alias
instead of the default. When omitted, snatcher uses the
.B SNATCHER_SERVER
environment variable if set, otherwise the configured default server. This
option is accepted only by commands that connect to a server; it never appears
on the named-server registry subcommands
.RB ( server\ add ", " server\ list ", " server\ default ", " server\ show ", " server\ ping ", " server\ remove ),
.BR version ,
or
.BR completion .
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit JSON instead of plaintext.
.TP
.BR \-\-jq " " \fIexpr\fR
Filter JSON output through the jq expression
.IR expr .
Implies JSON and takes precedence over
.BR \-\-json .
.SH COMMANDS
.SS "server"
Manage configured servers and the server itself. The
.B server
group maintains an inventory of named servers \(em the registry:
.BR add ", " list ", " default ", " show ", " ping ", " remove
\(em and operates on the selected server's session settings, statistics,
BitTorrent listening port, and download-path free space
.RB ( settings ", " stats ", " port ", " free-space ).
.TP
.B snatcher server add \fR[\fIoptions\fR]
Add or update a named server. Its URL and username are stored in the config
file under the server's alias and the password in a secret store, by default
the OS keyring. The password is read from a hidden, non-echoing prompt (with
confirmation) or, for scripting, from standard input with
.BR \-\-password\-stdin .
An empty password is a valid choice (a server with authentication disabled)
and stores no secret. When the alias's URL or its secret store changes, the
secret it used to keep is deleted from the store that held it, so no password
is left behind in a store the server no longer uses. The first server
registered when no default is set becomes the default. Options:
.RS
.TP
.BR \-\-name " " \fIalias\fR
Alias the server is stored under. When omitted it defaults to the lowercased
URL host (including the port).
.TP
.BR \-\-url " " \fIurl\fR
Transmission RPC URL, e.g.\&
.IR http://host:9091/transmission/rpc .
Required. Must be an absolute http or https URL with no embedded credentials.
.TP
.BR \-\-username " " \fIname\fR
Username for HTTP Basic Auth.
.TP
.B \-\-password\-stdin
Read the password from standard input instead of prompting.
.TP
.BR \-\-store " " \fIname\fR
Secret store this server's password lives in, one of
.BR keyring ", " file ", or " none .
.B keyring
is the default: the operating system keyring, and only the keyring \(em it
never falls back to another store when it cannot be reached.
.B file
writes the password in
.B plaintext
to
.IR $XDG_STATE_HOME/snatcher/secrets.json ,
created with mode 0600 under a 0700 directory; it exists for machines with no
OS keyring and is less safe than the keyring.
.B none
stores nothing at all. Whichever store is chosen is both written to and saved
with the server, so later commands use the same store without repeating the
option. When the option is omitted the store is resolved the usual way \(em
.B SNATCHER_SECRET_STORE
first, then the store the alias already had, then
.B keyring
\(em and that result is saved. An explicit
.B \-\-store
outranks
.BR SNATCHER_SECRET_STORE ,
which is the one place it does; see
.BR ENVIRONMENT .
.RE
.TP
.B snatcher server list \fR[\fIoptions\fR]
List every configured server, sorted by alias, showing its name, URL, username,
whether a secret is stored, and whether it is the default. Secret status is
read from the store each server itself names
.RB ( secret_store ),
not the one
.B SNATCHER_SECRET_STORE
selects for this invocation, so an exported override cannot report a stored
password as missing. Plaintext is
an aligned table on a terminal and tab-separated, header-less rows when piped or
when
.B NO_COLOR
is set (the same contract as
.BR list ).
Accepts
.BR \-\-config ", " \-\-json ", and " \-\-jq .
.TP
.B snatcher server default \fIalias\fR \fR[\fIoptions\fR]
Set the default server to the existing alias
.IR alias .
The default is used by every command that connects to a server when
.B \-\-server
and
.B SNATCHER_SERVER
are not given. An unknown alias is rejected, listing the configured names.
Accepts
.BR \-\-config .
.TP
.B snatcher server show \fR[\fIalias\fR] \fR[\fIoptions\fR]
Show everything about a server: its local connection settings (name, URL,
username, resolved config path, whether a secret is stored, whether it is
the default), its live identity (version, RPC semver, negotiated protocol), and
every session setting. Shows the named server
.IR alias ,
or the configured default when the alias is omitted. The secret value itself is
never revealed, and whether one is stored is read from the store the server
itself names, as in
.BR "snatcher server list" .
.IP
Identity and settings are two projections of one
.B session\-get
response, so the whole view costs a single round-trip. A first run with no
servers configured reports the empty connection block rather than an error, and
never dials.
.IP
Plaintext is an aligned
.RI Label : " value"
connection block, a blank line, then the settings as a
.BR CATEGORY " " KEY " " VALUE
table (the same terminal/piped contract as
.BR list ).
When piped or when
.B NO_COLOR
is set, the connection block becomes header-less
.RI key <TAB> value
lines using kebab-case keys, followed directly by the tab-separated settings
rows. Speed limits
.RB ( speed\-limit\-down ", " speed\-limit\-up ", " alt\-speed\-down ", " alt\-speed\-up )
are in KB/s; the alt-speed schedule times
.RB ( alt\-speed\-time\-begin ", " alt\-speed\-time\-end )
are minutes after midnight (e.g.\& 540 is 09:00). Use
.B \-\-jq
to read a single value in a script, e.g.\&
.BR "port=$(snatcher server show --jq '.settings[]|select(.key==\(dqpeer-port\(dq).value')" .
Besides
.BR \-\-config ", " \-\-json ", and " \-\-jq ,
it accepts:
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-local
Show only the local connection settings, without contacting the server. Cannot
be combined with
.BR \-\-category ", " \-\-search ", or " \-\-fields .
.TP
.BR \-\-category " " \fIname\fR
Show only settings in one category. One of
.BR download ", " network ", " bandwidth ", " queue .
Applies to every output mode.
.TP
.BR \-\-search " " \fItext\fR
Show only settings whose key contains
.I text
(case-insensitive). Applies to every output mode.
.TP
.BR \-\-fields " " \fIlist\fR
Comma-separated keys to show, naming connection fields
.RB ( name ", " server\-url ", " username ", " config\-path ", " secret\-stored ", "
.BR is\-default ", " version ", " rpc\-version\-semver ", " protocol ),
setting keys, or both. It selects without reordering \(em each section keeps
its canonical order \(em has no effect on
.B \-\-json
(always the full object) or
.B \-\-jq
(which selects fields itself), and cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-category " or " \-\-search .
.RE
.TP
.B snatcher server ping \fR[\fIalias\fR] \fR[\fIoptions\fR]
Dial a server and perform a real round-trip, reporting the connection status,
server version, negotiated protocol, and RPC semver. Pings the named server
.IR alias ,
or the configured default when the alias is omitted. Exits non-zero on failure.
Accepts
.BR \-\-config " and " \-\-json .
.TP
.B snatcher server remove \fR[\fIalias\fR] \fR[\fIoptions\fR]
Remove a configured server: delete its stored secret, drop its entry from the
config file, and persist. Removes the named server
.IR alias ,
or the configured default when the alias is omitted. The secret is deleted from
the store the server itself names
.RB ( secret_store ),
not the one
.B SNATCHER_SECRET_STORE
selects for this invocation, so an exported override cannot leave a password
behind in a store nothing points at any more. When the removed server was
the default, the default is repointed to the sole remaining server if exactly
one remains, otherwise it is cleared. An unknown alias is rejected. Accepts
.BR \-\-config .
.TP
.B snatcher server stats \fR[\fIoptions\fR]
Show session statistics: torrent counts, current download and upload speeds,
and cumulative and current-session transfer totals. Accepts the common options
only.
.TP
.B snatcher server update \fR[\fIoptions\fR]
Change one or more server (session) settings, each given as a typed flag whose
name is the setting key (e.g.\&
.BR \-\-peer\-port " " 51413 " " \-\-dht\-enabled = false ).
Only the flags you pass are written, so unspecified settings are left
unchanged; at least one setting flag is required (a bare invocation is an error,
not a no-op). Bool settings are enabled with a bare
.B \-\-key
and disabled with
.BR \-\-key = false .
Enum flags are validated before any network call, so an invalid value aborts
with no partial change. After writing, the values you set are echoed back \(em
this is what was written, not a re-read, so server-side normalization (e.g.\&
clamping an out-of-range port) is not reflected. Speed limits
.RB ( speed\-limit\-down ", " speed\-limit\-up ", " alt\-speed\-down ", " alt\-speed\-up )
are in KB/s; the alt-speed schedule times
.RB ( alt\-speed\-time\-begin ", " alt\-speed\-time\-end )
are minutes after midnight (e.g.\& 540 is 09:00). Read the settings back with
.BR "snatcher server show" ,
and restore defaults with
.BR "snatcher server reset" .
The read-only
.B blocklist\-size
has no flag. Besides the common
.BR \-\-config ", " \-\-server ", " \-\-json ", and " \-\-jq
options, one flag exists per settable setting. Download:
.RS
.TP
.BR \-\-download\-dir " " \fIpath\fR
Default directory for completed downloads.
.TP
.BR \-\-start\-added\-torrents [= true | false ]
Start torrents automatically when added.
.TP
.BR \-\-rename\-partial\-files [= true | false ]
Append
.B .part
to incomplete files.
.TP
.BR \-\-incomplete\-dir\-enabled [= true | false ]
Keep incomplete downloads in a separate directory.
.TP
.BR \-\-incomplete\-dir " " \fIpath\fR
Directory for incomplete downloads (when enabled).
.TP
.BR \-\-seed\-ratio\-limited [= true | false ]
Stop seeding at the global seed-ratio limit.
.TP
.BR \-\-seed\-ratio\-limit " " \fIratio\fR
Global seed ratio to stop seeding at (when limited).
.TP
.BR \-\-idle\-seeding\-limit\-enabled [= true | false ]
Stop seeding torrents that have been idle too long.
.TP
.BR \-\-idle\-seeding\-limit " " \fIminutes\fR
Idle time in minutes before stopping seeding (when enabled).
.TP
.BR \-\-cache\-size\-mib " " \fIn\fR
Disk cache size in MiB.
.RE
.IP
Network:
.RS
.TP
.BR \-\-peer\-port " " \fIn\fR
Port to listen for incoming peer connections.
.TP
.BR \-\-peer\-port\-random\-on\-start [= true | false ]
Pick a random peer port on each start.
.TP
.BR \-\-port\-forwarding\-enabled [= true | false ]
Request UPnP/NAT-PMP port forwarding.
.TP
.BR \-\-encryption " " \fIallowed | preferred | required\fR
Peer encryption mode.
.TP
.BR \-\-peer\-limit\-global " " \fIn\fR
Maximum peer connections across all torrents.
.TP
.BR \-\-peer\-limit\-per\-torrent " " \fIn\fR
Maximum peer connections per torrent.
.TP
.BR \-\-pex\-enabled [= true | false ]
Enable Peer Exchange (PEX).
.TP
.BR \-\-dht\-enabled [= true | false ]
Enable the Distributed Hash Table (DHT).
.TP
.BR \-\-lpd\-enabled [= true | false ]
Enable Local Peer Discovery (LPD).
.TP
.BR \-\-utp\-enabled [= true | false ]
Enable the uTP transport protocol.
.TP
.BR \-\-blocklist\-enabled [= true | false ]
Block peers listed in the blocklist.
.TP
.BR \-\-blocklist\-url " " \fIurl\fR
URL to fetch the peer blocklist from.
.RE
.IP
Bandwidth (the speed limits are in KB/s; the schedule times are minutes after
midnight):
.RS
.TP
.BR \-\-speed\-limit\-down\-enabled [= true | false ]
Enable the global download speed limit.
.TP
.BR \-\-speed\-limit\-down " " \fIKBps\fR
Global download speed limit in KB/s (when enabled).
.TP
.BR \-\-speed\-limit\-up\-enabled [= true | false ]
Enable the global upload speed limit.
.TP
.BR \-\-speed\-limit\-up " " \fIKBps\fR
Global upload speed limit in KB/s (when enabled).
.TP
.BR \-\-alt\-speed\-enabled [= true | false ]
Use the alternative (throttled) speed limits now.
.TP
.BR \-\-alt\-speed\-down " " \fIKBps\fR
Alternative download speed limit in KB/s.
.TP
.BR \-\-alt\-speed\-up " " \fIKBps\fR
Alternative upload speed limit in KB/s.
.TP
.BR \-\-alt\-speed\-time\-enabled [= true | false ]
Switch to alternative speeds on a daily schedule.
.TP
.BR \-\-alt\-speed\-time\-begin " " \fIminutes\fR
Alternative-speed schedule start (minutes after midnight).
.TP
.BR \-\-alt\-speed\-time\-end " " \fIminutes\fR
Alternative-speed schedule end (minutes after midnight).
.TP
.BR \-\-alt\-speed\-time\-day " " \fIn\fR
Days the alternative-speed schedule runs (bitfield, Sun=1).
.RE
.IP
Queue:
.RS
.TP
.BR \-\-download\-queue\-enabled [= true | false ]
Limit how many torrents download at once.
.TP
.BR \-\-download\-queue\-size " " \fIn\fR
Maximum torrents downloading at once (when enabled).
.TP
.BR \-\-seed\-queue\-enabled [= true | false ]
Limit how many torrents seed at once.
.TP
.BR \-\-seed\-queue\-size " " \fIn\fR
Maximum torrents seeding at once (when enabled).
.TP
.BR \-\-queue\-stalled\-enabled [= true | false ]
Ignore stalled torrents when counting the queue.
.TP
.BR \-\-queue\-stalled\-minutes " " \fIn\fR
Idle minutes before a transfer counts as stalled.
.RE
.TP
.B snatcher server reset \fIkey\fR...
Restore one or more settings to their Transmission defaults. Every key is
validated before anything is sent, so an unknown or read-only key (the only
read-only setting is
.BR blocklist\-size )
aborts the whole batch with no partial change; passing no key is an error, not
a no-op. Every default travels in one write, after which the settings are
re-read and the server's confirmed values are reported \(em the same output
shape as
.BR "snatcher server update" ,
in registry order regardless of the order the keys were named. Accepts
.BR \-\-config ", " \-\-server ", " \-\-json ", and " \-\-jq .
.TP
.B snatcher server port \fR[\fIoptions\fR]
View, change, and test the BitTorrent peer/listening port \(em a discoverable
wrapper over the peer-port session settings. With no subcommand it prints help.
.TP
.B snatcher server port get \fR[\fIoptions\fR]
Show the current peer port alongside the random-on-start and port-forwarding
flags read from the live session. Plaintext is an aligned
.RI Label : " value"
block;
.B \-\-json
emits a stable
.RB { port ", " random_on_start ", " forwarding_enabled }
object. Accepts
.BR \-\-config ", " \-\-json ", and " \-\-jq .
.TP
.B snatcher server port set \fR[\fIport\fR] [\fIoptions\fR]
Set the peer port and/or related flags, then re-read the session and report the
resulting state in the same shape as
.BR "server port get" .
A
.I port
must be an integer in
.RB [ 1 ", " 65535 ];
giving one also disables
.B random_on_start
so the port sticks across restarts, unless
.B \-\-random
is passed explicitly. Besides the common options:
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-random\fR[=\fIbool\fR]
Randomize the peer port on each server start. A bare
.B \-\-random
means true; an explicit flag overrides the auto-disable triggered by a
.I port
argument.
.TP
.B \-\-forwarding\fR[=\fIbool\fR]
Enable or disable port forwarding (UPnP/NAT-PMP).
.RE
.IP
With neither a
.I port
nor any flag, it returns a "nothing to set" error. Accepts
.BR \-\-config ", " \-\-json ", and " \-\-jq .
.TP
.B snatcher server port test \fR[\fIoptions\fR]
Test whether the currently configured peer port is reachable from outside (it
takes no
.I port
argument). Reports
.B open
or
.B closed
in plaintext and emits an
.RB { open ", " ip_protocol }
object under
.BR \-\-json
(\c
.B ip_protocol
is reported only by Transmission's JSON-RPC 2.0 scheme and omitted otherwise).
Exits 0 when the port is open and non-zero when it is closed, while still
writing the structured result to stdout; a connection or authentication failure
is a distinct non-zero exit that renders no result. Accepts
.BR \-\-config ", " \-\-json ", and " \-\-jq .
.TP
.B snatcher server free-space \fR[\fIpath\fR] [\fIoptions\fR]
Show the free space on a download path. With no
.I path
it queries the server's default download directory. Plaintext is an aligned
.RI Label : " value"
block;
.B \-\-json
emits a stable
.RB { path ", " size_bytes ", " total_bytes }
object. The
.B total_bytes
total capacity is reported only by Transmission 4.1+ and omitted otherwise (both
in the object and the plaintext block). Accepts
.BR \-\-config ", " \-\-json ", and " \-\-jq .
.SS "torrent"
Manage individual torrents. All torrent operations live under the
.B torrent
command group:
.BR add ", " show ", " files ", " peers ", " trackers ", " list ", " start ", " stop ", " verify ", " reannounce ", " move ", " rename ", " queue ", " remove ", and " update .
The mutation subcommands
.RB ( start ", " stop ", " verify ", " reannounce ", " remove )
select torrents by numeric id, 40-character SHA1 hash, or
.BR \-\-all ;
at least one selector is required, so a bare invocation is an error, not a
no-op. Every subcommand accepts the common options.
.TP
.B snatcher torrent add \fR[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIfile | url | magnet\fR]...
Add torrents to the server from local
.I .torrent
files, HTTP(S) URLs, or magnet links. Each argument is auto-detected as a
file, URL, or magnet link. With no positional arguments, sources are read from
standard input, one per line; a bare
.B snatcher torrent add
with no sources and no piped input is an error. Multiple sources are processed
continue-on-error: a failure adding one source is reported but does not stop
the rest, and the command exits non-zero if any source failed. Besides the
common options:
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-start
Start torrents immediately instead of adding them paused.
.TP
.BR \-\-download\-dir " " \fIpath\fR
Server-side destination directory.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-delete
Delete the local
.I .torrent
after a successful, non-duplicate file add.
.TP
.BR \-\-label " " \fIlabel\fR
Label to apply to added torrents. May be repeated.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Suppress per-source confirmation lines (plaintext only; never alters JSON/jq
output).
.RE
.TP
.B snatcher torrent show \fR[\fIoptions\fR] \fIid | hash\fR
.RB ( info )
Show the full set of fields for a single torrent, identified by its numeric id
or 40-character SHA1 info-hash. Exactly one selector is required. On a terminal
the output is an aligned
.IR key ": " value
block; when piped (or when
.B NO_COLOR
is set) it is
.IR key <TAB> value
lines with no header \(em a stable scripting contract. When
.B \-\-fields
selects exactly one field the piped form drops the key column too, printing
the bare value on its own line so it can be captured verbatim. With
.B \-\-json
the torrent is emitted as a single JSON object (not an array), and
.B \-\-jq
filters that object. Besides the common options:
.RS
.TP
.BR \-\-fields " " \fIlist\fR
Comma-separated fields to show in the plaintext output, in the given order.
When omitted every field except
.B magnet_link
is shown. Available fields:
.BR id ", " hash ", " name ", " status ", " done ", " size ", " down ,
.BR up ", " ratio ", " eta ", " peers ", " added ", " download_dir ,
.BR error ", " labels ", " comment ", " creator ", " date_created ,
.BR is_private ", " piece_count ", " piece_size ", " downloaded_ever ,
.BR uploaded_ever ", " corrupt_ever ", " have_valid ,
.BR seconds_downloading ", " seconds_seeding ", " recheck_progress ,
.BR metadata_percent_complete ", " magnet_link .
.IP
.B magnet_link
is opt-in: it is selectable but excluded from the default set, because a magnet
URI is a single long line that would dominate the block. Select it on its own
to print just the magnet URI \(em no label, no tab \(em e.g.
.IP
.EX
snatcher torrent show \-\-fields magnet_link 3 | pbcopy
.EE
.IP
This affects plaintext only:
.B \-\-json
always emits the full object and
.B \-\-jq
selects fields itself.
.IP
A magnet URI reproduces the torrent's announce URLs in its
.B tr=
parameters, and a private tracker's announce URL commonly embeds a per-user
passkey, so treat
.B magnet_link
output as a credential: do not paste it verbatim into a public issue report or a
pastebin. Nothing is redacted \(em that would break the scripting contract \(em
so the redaction is yours to do. See
.B "snatcher torrent trackers"
for the same caution on the announce URLs themselves.
.RE
.TP
.B snatcher torrent files \fR[\fIoptions\fR] \fIid | hash\fR
List the files inside a single torrent, identified by its numeric id or
40-character SHA1 info-hash. Exactly one selector is required, so a bare
.B snatcher torrent files
is an error, not a no-op. On a terminal the output is an aligned table with a
header; when piped (or when
.B NO_COLOR
is set) it is tab-separated, full-value rows with no header \(em a stable
scripting contract. A torrent with no files prints
.B "No files."
on a terminal and nothing when piped. With
.B \-\-json
the files are emitted as an array in torrent order, so an element's array
position is its
.B index
column.
.IP
File indices are
.BR 0 -based
and torrent-relative: the
.B index
column is the file's position within this torrent, counting from zero, and is
the same index space the per-file selection and priority settings use. Besides
the common options:
.RS
.TP
.BR \-\-fields " " \fIlist\fR
Comma-separated columns to show, in the given order. When omitted every column
is shown. Available columns:
.BR index ", " name ", " size ", " done ", " percent ", " priority ", " wanted .
.RE
.TP
.B snatcher torrent files update \fR[\fIoptions\fR] \fIid | hash\fR \fIindex\fR...
Change the download priority and the wanted/unwanted selection of individual
files inside a single torrent. Exactly one torrent is selected, by numeric id or
40-character SHA1 info-hash; file indices are torrent-relative, so an index set
cannot meaningfully span several torrents and none is accepted.
.IP
Indices are
.BR 0 -based
and are the same numbers the
.B index
column of
.B "snatcher torrent files"
prints. One or more
.I index
arguments follow the selector, and each may hold several comma-separated
tokens. A token is a single index
.RB ( 3 ),
an inclusive range
.RB ( 2\-7 ),
or the literal
.BR all .
Tokens may be mixed freely across arguments, e.g.
.BR "snatcher torrent files update 5 0,2\-4 9" ;
.B all
selects every file in the torrent and must be given on its own. A reversed
range, a negative or non-integer index, and an empty token are each errors
naming the offending argument. Every index is validated against the torrent's
file count before anything is written, so an out-of-range index leaves the
torrent untouched. A torrent with no files \(em a magnet whose metadata has not
arrived yet, for instance \(em rejects every index form,
.B all
included, rather than reporting a change that could not have happened.
.IP
At least one of
.BR \-\-priority ", " \-\-wanted ", or " \-\-unwanted
is required; an invocation with none of them is an error, not a no-op. After the
change the affected files are re-read from the server and printed in the same
columns, and with the same
.B \-\-fields
handling, as
.BR "snatcher torrent files" .
Besides the common options:
.RS
.TP
.BR \-\-priority " " \fIlevel\fR
Download priority for the selected files. One of
.BR low ", " normal ", " high .
.TP
.B \-\-wanted
Download the selected files. This is a switch, not a boolean setting:
.B \-\-wanted=false
is not a way to mark a file unwanted. It reads as "not given", so an invocation
carrying only that fails with the "nothing to change" error and writes nothing.
Use
.B \-\-unwanted
to deselect a file.
.TP
.B \-\-unwanted
Do not download the selected files. Mutually exclusive with
.BR \-\-wanted ,
and a switch in the same way:
.B \-\-unwanted=false
is not a way to mark a file wanted; use
.BR \-\-wanted .
.TP
.BR \-\-fields " " \fIlist\fR
Comma-separated columns to show, in the given order \(em the same columns as
.BR "snatcher torrent files" :
.BR index ", " name ", " size ", " done ", " percent ", " priority ", " wanted .
.RE
.TP
.B snatcher torrent peers \fR[\fIoptions\fR] \fIid | hash\fR
List the peers a single torrent is currently connected to, identified by its
numeric id or 40-character SHA1 info-hash. Exactly one selector is required, so a
bare
.B snatcher torrent peers
is an error, not a no-op. On a terminal the output is an aligned table with a
header; when piped (or when
.B NO_COLOR
is set) it is tab-separated, full-value rows with no header \(em a stable
scripting contract. A torrent with no connected peers prints
.B "No peers."
on a terminal and nothing when piped. With
.B \-\-json
the peers are emitted as an array in the server's order.
.IP
The
.B client
and
.B status
columns are chosen by the remote peer, not by the server, so they are
neutralized of terminal control sequences in the plaintext output;
.B \-\-json
and
.B \-\-jq
emit them verbatim. Besides the common options:
.RS
.TP
.BR \-\-fields " " \fIlist\fR
Comma-separated columns to show, in the given order. When omitted every column
is shown. Available columns:
.BR address ", " port ", " client ", " progress ", " down ", " up ,
.BR encrypted ", " status .
.RE
.TP
.B snatcher torrent trackers \fR[\fIoptions\fR] \fIid | hash\fR
List a single torrent's trackers and their per-tracker announce stats,
identified by its numeric id or 40-character SHA1 info-hash. Exactly one
selector is required, so a bare
.B snatcher torrent trackers
is an error, not a no-op. On a terminal the output is an aligned table with a
header; when piped (or when
.B NO_COLOR
is set) it is tab-separated, full-value rows with no header \(em a stable
scripting contract. A torrent with no trackers prints
.B "No trackers."
on a terminal and nothing when piped. With
.B \-\-json
the trackers are emitted as an array in the server's order.
.IP
The
.B state
column names the announce state
.RB ( inactive ", " waiting ", " queued ", " active );
.B \-\-json
emits the underlying integer. Transmission reports
.B \-1
for a seeder, leecher, or download count it has not scraped yet; those render as
.B \-
in the plaintext columns and stay
.B \-1
in
.BR \-\-json .
Announce URLs, hosts, site names, and last announce results come from the
torrent's metadata or from the tracker itself, not from the operator, so they
are neutralized of terminal control sequences in the plaintext output;
.B \-\-json
and
.B \-\-jq
emit them verbatim.
.IP
A private tracker's announce URL commonly embeds a per-user passkey, and
.B announce
is one of the default columns, so treat this output as a credential: do not
paste it verbatim into a public issue report or a pastebin. Nothing is redacted
\(em that would break the scripting contract \(em so the redaction is yours to
do. The same applies to
.BR "snatcher torrent show \-\-fields magnet_link" ,
whose
.B tr=
parameters reproduce these URLs. Besides the common options:
.RS
.TP
.BR \-\-fields " " \fIlist\fR
Comma-separated columns to show, in the given order. The default set is
.BR announce ", " tier ", " state ", " next_announce ", " seeders ,
.BR leechers ", " downloads .
Also available, but not shown by default:
.BR host ", " sitename ", " last_result .
.RE
.TP
.B snatcher torrent list \fR[\fIoptions\fR]
.RB ( ls )
List torrents. On a terminal the output is an aligned table with a header; when
piped (or when
.B NO_COLOR
is set) it is tab-separated, full-value rows with no header \(em a stable
scripting contract. Besides the common options:
.RS
.TP
.BR \-\-filter " " \fIstatus\fR
Filter by status (default
.BR all ).
One of
.BR all ", " active ", " downloading ", " seeding ", " stopped ,
.BR verifying ", " queued\-to\-verify ", " queued\-to\-download ,
.BR queued\-to\-seed ", " error .
.TP
.BR \-\-search " " \fItext\fR
Show only torrents whose name contains
.I text
(case-insensitive).
.TP
.BR \-\-sort " " \fIfield\fR
Sort by field (default
.BR queue ).
One of
.BR queue ", " name ", " added ", " size ", " progress ", " ratio ", " speed .
.TP
.B \-\-reverse
Reverse the sort order.
.TP
.BR \-\-fields " " \fIlist\fR
Comma-separated columns to show. The default set is
.BR id , name , status , done , size , down , up , ratio , eta .
Available columns:
.BR id ", " hash ", " name ", " status ", " done ", " size ", " down ,
.BR up ", " ratio ", " eta ", " peers ", " added ", " download_dir ,
.BR error ", " labels .
.RE
.TP
.B snatcher torrent start \fR[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIid | hash\fR]...
Start or resume the selected torrents. Besides the common options:
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-all
Start every torrent.
.TP
.B \-\-now
Bypass the queue and start immediately.
.RE
.TP
.B snatcher torrent stop \fR[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIid | hash\fR]...
.RB ( pause )
Stop (pause) the selected torrents. Besides the common options:
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-all
Stop every torrent.
.RE
.TP
.B snatcher torrent verify \fR[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIid | hash\fR]...
Verify local torrent data by queueing a re-hash of the downloaded files against
the torrent metadata. Besides the common options:
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-all
Verify every torrent.
.RE
.TP
.B snatcher torrent reannounce \fR[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIid | hash\fR]...
Reannounce the selected torrents to their trackers immediately, rather than
waiting for the next scheduled announce interval. Besides the common options:
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-all
Reannounce every torrent.
.RE
.TP
.B snatcher torrent move \fR[\fIoptions\fR] \fIid | hash\fR \fIlocation\fR
.RB ( mv )
Move a single torrent's download location. Select the torrent by numeric id or
40-character SHA1 hash. By default the existing files are relocated to
.IR location .
Besides the common options:
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-in\-place
Repoint Transmission at a path where the files already exist (after a manual
move) without relocating them.
.RE
.TP
.B snatcher torrent rename \fR[\fIoptions\fR] \fIid | hash\fR \fIpath\fR \fInew-name\fR
Rename a file or directory inside a single torrent. Select the torrent by
numeric id or 40-character SHA1 hash.
.I path
is the torrent-relative path of a file
.I or
directory, exactly as shown by
.BR "snatcher torrent files" ;
.I new-name
replaces its last component.
.IP
Renaming the torrent's root path
.RB ( path
equal to the torrent's own name) renames the torrent itself. That case prompts
.RB [ y/N ]
on a terminal, and when standard input is not a terminal it is refused unless
.B \-\-yes
is given. After a root rename, later paths are relative to the new root.
Besides the common options:
.RS
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Skip the confirmation prompt.
.RE
.TP
.B snatcher torrent queue \fR[\fIid | hash\fR]...
Reorder the selected torrents within the download/seed queue. Select torrents by
numeric id or 40-character SHA1 hash; at least one selector is required (there
is no
.B \-\-all
flag, and a bare invocation is an error, not a no-op). Each subcommand accepts
the common options and confirms by naming the affected torrents. With no
subcommand it prints help.
.RS
.TP
.B snatcher torrent queue top \fR[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIid | hash\fR]...
Move the selected torrents to the top (front) of the queue.
.TP
.B snatcher torrent queue up \fR[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIid | hash\fR]...
Move the selected torrents up one position in the queue.
.TP
.B snatcher torrent queue down \fR[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIid | hash\fR]...
Move the selected torrents down one position in the queue.
.TP
.B snatcher torrent queue bottom \fR[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIid | hash\fR]...
Move the selected torrents to the bottom (back) of the queue.
.RE
.TP
.B snatcher torrent remove \fR[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIid | hash\fR]...
.RB ( rm )
Remove the selected torrents from the session. Removal is destructive and
requires confirmation: on a terminal it prompts
.RB [ y/N ];
when standard input is not a terminal it refuses unless
.B \-\-yes
is given. Besides the common options:
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-all
Remove every torrent.
.TP
.B \-\-delete\-data
Also delete the downloaded local files.
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Skip the confirmation prompt.
.RE
.TP
.B snatcher torrent update \fR[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIid | hash\fR]...
Change mutable per-torrent fields on the selected torrents. Select torrents by
numeric id, 40-character SHA1 hash, or
.BR \-\-all .
At least one selector and at least one field flag are required; a bare
invocation is an error, not a no-op. Only the fields whose flags are explicitly
given are written, so unspecified fields are left unchanged. Besides the common
options:
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-all
Update the fields on every torrent.
.TP
.BR \-\-priority " " \fIlow | normal | high\fR
Bandwidth priority for the torrent.
.TP
.BR \-\-queue\-position " " \fIn\fR
Position in the download/seed queue (0-based).
.TP
.BR \-\-peer\-limit " " \fIn\fR
Maximum number of peers for the torrent (1 or greater).
.TP
.BR \-\-honors\-session\-limits [= true | false ]
Whether the torrent honors the global session speed limits.
.TP
.BR \-\-sequential\-download [= true | false ]
Download pieces in sequential order (Transmission 4.1+).
.TP
.BR \-\-download\-limit " " \fIkBps | off\fR
Per-torrent download speed limit in kB/s, enabling the limit; the literal
.B off
disables it.
.TP
.BR \-\-upload\-limit " " \fIkBps | off\fR
Per-torrent upload speed limit in kB/s, enabling the limit; the literal
.B off
disables it.
.TP
.BR \-\-seed\-ratio " " \fIratio | global | unlimited\fR
Per-torrent seed ratio limit. A number sets the limit;
.B global
follows the session default;
.B unlimited
seeds without a ratio limit.
.TP
.BR \-\-seed\-idle " " \fIminutes | duration | global | unlimited\fR
Per-torrent idle-seeding limit, as whole minutes (a bare integer or a Go-style
duration such as
.IR 30m ", " 1h ", or " 1h30m );
.B global
follows the session default;
.B unlimited
seeds without an idle limit.
.TP
.BR \-\-label " " \fIname\fR
Label to apply, replacing the torrent's entire label set. May be repeated.
Mutually exclusive with
.BR \-\-clear\-labels .
.TP
.B \-\-clear\-labels
Remove all labels from the torrent.
.TP
.BR \-\-tracker " " \fIurl\fR
Announce URL, replacing the torrent's entire tracker list. May be repeated.
Mutually exclusive with
.BR \-\-clear\-trackers .
.TP
.B \-\-clear\-trackers
Remove all trackers from the torrent.
.TP
.BR \-\-group " " \fIname\fR
Bandwidth group name for the torrent.
.RE
.SS "config"
Read snatcher's own settings \(em how snatcher itself behaves, as opposed to
which server it talks to. They live in the
.B client
object of the config file (see
.BR FILES )
and are addressed on the command line by dotted keys such as
.BR client.theme .
The
.B config
group covers that section only; named servers are managed with
.B snatcher server
instead.
.TP
.B snatcher config list \fR[\fIoptions\fR]
List every client setting, sorted by key, with its effective value and the
source that value came from:
.B config
when the key is set in the config file, and
.B default
when it is not. Plaintext is an aligned KEY VALUE SOURCE table on a terminal and
tab-separated, header-less rows when piped or when
.B NO_COLOR
is set (the same contract as
.BR list ).
Accepts
.BR \-\-config ", " \-\-json ", and " \-\-jq .
.TP
.B snatcher config get \fIkey\fR \fR[\fIoptions\fR]
Print the effective value of one setting. The plaintext output is the bare value
with nothing else \(em no key name, padding, or decoration \(em so it can be
captured directly in a shell, e.g.\&
.IR "theme=$(snatcher config get client.theme)" ;
a list-valued key prints comma-separated.
.B \-\-json
and
.B \-\-jq
emit the same
.IR key ", " value ", " source
object that
.B config list
emits per row, with the value in its natural JSON type. An unknown key is an
error listing the valid keys. A value in the config file that is not valid for
its key is an error naming the key, the offending value, and what is accepted;
it is never silently replaced by the default. Accepts
.BR \-\-config ", " \-\-json ", and " \-\-jq .
.PP
The settings, with the defaults that apply when they are not set:
.TP
.B client.theme
Color theme, one of
.BR default ", " dracula ", or " nord .
Default:
.BR default .
.TP
.B client.nerd_font
Whether to draw Nerd Font glyphs rather than ASCII icons
.RB ( true / false ).
Default:
.BR true .
.TP
.B client.poll_interval
How often the interface refreshes its data, in Go duration syntax (e.g.\&
.IR 5s ", " 1m30s ),
between 1s and 1h. Default:
.BR 5s .
.TP
.B client.list_columns
Comma-separated default columns for
.BR "torrent list" ,
in order, named from the same set as its
.B \-\-fields
option. Default:
.BR id,name,status,done,size,down,up,ratio,eta .
.TP
.B client.list_sort
Default sort field for
.BR "torrent list" ,
named from the same set as its
.B \-\-sort
option. Default:
.BR queue .
.TP
.B client.list_reverse
Whether
.B "torrent list"
reverses its sort order by default
.RB ( true / false ).
Default:
.BR false .
.PP
.BR client.theme ", " client.nerd_font ", and " client.poll_interval
are stored and validated but nothing renders them yet; they exist for the
forthcoming TUI.
.SS "api"
.B snatcher api
.RI [ options "] " method
.br
Power-user escape hatch, modeled on
.BR "gh api" ,
that issues an arbitrary Transmission RPC
.I method
(canonical kebab-case, e.g.\&
.IR session\-get ", " torrent\-get ", " queue\-move\-top )
and prints a normalized response envelope that is identical whether the server
speaks the bespoke dialect (Transmission 4.0 and earlier) or JSON-RPC 2.0
(4.1 and later). snatcher translates the method name only; it never rewrites
argument keys or values, which are not portable across dialect versions. An
RPC-level failure still prints the envelope, then exits non-zero. Options:
.RS
.TP
.BR \-F ", " \-\-field " " \fIkey=value\fR
Add a type-inferred argument field:
.BR true ", " false ", and " null
become JSON literals, numbers become JSON numbers,
.IR @file " or " @\-
read a file or standard input as a string, and anything else is a string. The
.I key:=value
form parses the value as raw JSON. Repeatable.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-raw\-field " " \fIkey=value\fR
Add a string argument field; the value is always a literal string. Repeatable.
.TP
.BR \-\-input " " \fIfile\fR
Supply the entire arguments object as raw JSON from
.IR file ,
or
.B \-
for standard input. Mutually exclusive with
.BR \-\-field " and " \-\-raw\-field .
.TP
.B \-\-raw
Print the server's exact response bytes, bypassing normalization. Mutually
exclusive with
.BR \-\-jq .
.RE
.PP
The
.B api
command also accepts
.B \-\-config
and
.BR \-\-jq ;
it has no
.B \-\-json
flag, since the normalized envelope is already JSON.
.SS "version, completion, help"
.B snatcher version
prints version information.
.B snatcher completion
generates a shell completion script for bash, zsh, fish, or PowerShell.
.B snatcher help
.RI [ command ]
shows help for any command.
.SH OUTPUT
When a command can emit structured output, the render mode is chosen with this
precedence:
.BR \-\-jq " (highest), then " \-\-json ", then plaintext (default)."
The
.B json
field tags on the underlying types are a stable scripting surface; treat them
as an API. Human and plaintext output passes server-derived strings through a
terminal sanitizer; JSON and jq output does not, as it is a machine contract.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B non-zero
An error occurred: the server was unreachable, authentication failed, the
selectors matched no torrents, a confirmation was refused, or at least one
.B add
source failed.
.SH SECRETS
A server's password is the only secret snatcher holds, and it never goes in the
config file. Each server names the store its password lives in, chosen with
.B \-\-store
at
.B "snatcher server add"
time, saved as
.B secret_store
in the config file, and overridable for one invocation with
.BR SNATCHER_SECRET_STORE .
.TP
.B keyring
The operating system keyring (macOS Keychain, or the Secret Service on Linux),
under the service name
.B snatcher
keyed by the server URL. This is the default and the only store snatcher will
use unless told otherwise.
.TP
.B file
A
.B plaintext
JSON file at
.IR $XDG_STATE_HOME/snatcher/secrets.json ,
keyed by server URL, created with mode 0600 under a 0700 directory. It exists
for machines with no OS keyring and is
.B less safe
than the keyring: anything that can read the file (a backup, another process
running as you, a misconfigured sync) reads the password. Nothing writes to it
unless it is asked for by name.
.TP
.B none
No store at all: nothing is read and nothing is written. Supply the password
per invocation with
.B SNATCHER_PASSWORD
instead, or use a server with authentication disabled.
.PP
.B SNATCHER_PASSWORD
sits above every store on read paths, so it works in all three modes. It is
never written to any store.
.SS "When the secret store is unavailable"
Snatcher distinguishes
.I no entry for this server
from
.IR "the store could not be reached at all" .
The second is reported as the secret store being
.BR unavailable ,
and is expected on a headless Linux box: the Secret Service is reached over the
session D\-Bus, so with no D\-Bus session running there is nothing to answer,
and every keyring read and write fails. It is not a sign of a corrupt keyring
or a wrong password.
.PP
Read paths degrade rather than abort.
.BR "server list" ", " "server show" ", " "server remove" ,
and every command that connects to a server keep working; each prints a
one-line warning
on standard error and, where a password was needed, proceeds without one. If
the server does require authentication, the request then fails with the usual
authentication error.
.PP
.B "snatcher server add"
is the one exception, and only when a non-empty password was typed: it refuses
rather than register a server whose password went nowhere, and writes nothing
at all \(em no config entry and no secret. An empty password always succeeds,
since there is nothing to store. The three ways forward are:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
Install or start an OS secret service (on Linux, a Secret Service provider such
as gnome\-keyring, and a D\-Bus session for it to answer on).
.IP \(bu 2
Re-run with
.B \-\-store=file
to keep the password in the plaintext file store described above.
.IP \(bu 2
Re-run with
.B \-\-store=none
and supply the password per invocation with
.BR SNATCHER_PASSWORD ,
which leaves nothing at rest on disk.
.RE
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SNATCHER_SERVER
Selects the configured server alias to connect to, equivalent to passing
.BR \-\-server .
The
.B \-\-server
flag takes precedence; when neither is set, the configured default server is
used.
.TP
.B SNATCHER_SECRET_STORE
Selects the secret store passwords are read from and written to, for every
server, for this invocation. One of
.BR keyring ", " file ", or " none ,
with the same meanings as
.B \-\-store
on
.BR "snatcher server add" .
Precedence is
.B SNATCHER_SECRET_STORE
over the server's
.B secret_store
value in the config file, over
.BR keyring .
An unrecognized value is an error, never a silent fallback. An explicit
.B \-\-store
on
.B "snatcher server add"
outranks it, so the store saved with a server is always the one its password
was written to. It is also ignored wherever the question is about a secret a
server already has rather than about the password to use for this invocation:
.B "snatcher server remove"
and the clean-up
.B "snatcher server add"
performs when an alias's URL or store changes both delete from the store the
server itself names, since that is where the password actually is, and
.B "snatcher server list"
and
.B "snatcher server show"
report secret status from that same store, so an override cannot make a stored
password look absent.
.TP
.B SNATCHER_PASSWORD
Supplies the password for this invocation, ahead of every secret store. When it
is set to a non-empty value, no store is read at all, whatever
.B SNATCHER_SECRET_STORE
or the server's
.B secret_store
names, so
.B SNATCHER_SECRET_STORE=none
together with
.B SNATCHER_PASSWORD
is a fully unattended configuration with no password written to disk. It is a
read-only override:
.B "snatcher server add"
never stores its value, and an empty or unset value defers to the resolved
store.
.IP
Beware that a process environment is not private. On Linux any other process
running as the same user can read it (for example
.IR /proc/ PID /environ ),
it is inherited by every child process snatcher spawns, and it is commonly
captured in shell history and CI logs. Prefer the OS keyring where one is
available.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
When set to a non-empty value, forces
.B list
into the piped, tab-separated, header-less contract even on a terminal.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
When set, the default config path is
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/snatcher/config.json .
.TP
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
When set, the default path of the
.B file
secret store is
.IR $XDG_STATE_HOME/snatcher/secrets.json .
It is consulted only in the
.B file
store mode; the keyring never touches it.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/snatcher/config.json
Non-secret configuration (a
.B servers
map of named server aliases, each with a
.B url
and
.BR username ,
a
.B default_server
alias, and a
.B client
object holding snatcher's own settings \(em see
.BR "snatcher config" ),
created with mode 0600. The file is operator-owned and hand-editable: keys
snatcher does not recognize, at either level, survive a rewrite untouched.
Falls back to
.I ~/.config/snatcher/config.json
when
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is unset. Server passwords are never written to this file; it holds only the
name of the store each server's password lives in
.RB ( secret_store ).
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/snatcher/secrets.json
The
.B file
secret store: server passwords in
.BR plaintext ,
as a JSON object keyed by server URL. Written atomically with mode 0600 under a
0700 directory. Falls back to
.I ~/.local/state/snatcher/secrets.json
when
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
is unset. This file is created only for a server explicitly registered with
.BR \-\-store=file " (or under " SNATCHER_SECRET_STORE=file );
the default
.B keyring
store never creates it, and never falls back to it when the keyring cannot be
reached.
.PP
In the default
.B keyring
store the password is in no file at all: it lives in the operating system
keyring (macOS Keychain or the Linux Secret Service), under the service name
.B snatcher
keyed by the server URL. See
.B SECRETS
for how a store is chosen.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS "Configure and verify a server"
.EX
# point snatcher at your Transmission RPC endpoint (prompts for a password)
$ snatcher server add \-\-url http://localhost:9091/transmission/rpc \-\-username admin

# read the password from stdin instead of a prompt (scripting)
$ printf '%s' "$TRANSMISSION_PASSWORD" | snatcher server add \(rs
    \-\-url http://localhost:9091/transmission/rpc \-\-username admin \-\-password\-stdin

# confirm the connection works
$ snatcher server ping
.EE
.SS "List and filter torrents"
.EX
# seeding torrents, highest ratio first
$ snatcher torrent list \-\-filter seeding \-\-sort ratio \-\-reverse

# pick the columns you care about
$ snatcher torrent list \-\-fields id,name,ratio,up

# search by name
$ snatcher torrent list \-\-search ubuntu

# names of torrents that are not finished, via jq
$ snatcher torrent list \-\-jq '.[] | select(.done < 100) | .name'
.EE
.SS "Start, stop, and remove torrents"
.EX
# start three torrents by id, jumping the queue
$ snatcher torrent start 1 2 3 \-\-now

# pause everything
$ snatcher torrent stop \-\-all

# remove a torrent and its data without prompting
$ snatcher torrent remove 5 \-\-delete\-data \-\-yes
.EE
.SS "Add torrents"
.EX
# add a file and a magnet link, label them, and start immediately
$ snatcher torrent add ubuntu.iso.torrent 'magnet:?xt=urn:btih:...' \(rs
    \-\-label linux \-\-label iso \-\-start

# add every source listed in a file (one per line)
$ snatcher torrent add < sources.txt
.EE
.SS "Rename a file or a torrent"
.EX
# find the torrent\-relative path, then rename that file
$ snatcher torrent files 3
$ snatcher torrent rename 3 'Ubuntu 24.04/disk.iso' ubuntu.iso

# renaming the root path renames the torrent itself, so it prompts
$ snatcher torrent rename 3 'Ubuntu 24.04' ubuntu\-24.04

# same, unattended (required when stdin is not a terminal)
$ snatcher torrent rename 3 'Ubuntu 24.04' ubuntu\-24.04 \-\-yes
.EE
.SS "Session statistics"
.EX
$ snatcher server stats
.EE
.SS "Inspect and change settings"
.EX
# show the connection, the server version, and every setting
$ snatcher server show

# show only the bandwidth settings
$ snatcher server show \-\-category bandwidth

# show the connection settings without contacting the server
$ snatcher server show \-\-local

# read one value (script-friendly)
$ port=$(snatcher server show \-\-jq \(rs
    '.settings[]|select(.key=="peer\-port").value')

# set the download speed limit to 500 KB/s and enable it
$ snatcher server update \-\-speed\-limit\-down 500 \-\-speed\-limit\-down\-enabled

# restore settings to their defaults
$ snatcher server reset peer\-port encryption
.EE
.SS "Read snatcher's own settings"
.EX
# every client setting, its value, and where the value came from
$ snatcher config list

# one value, bare, for a shell
$ theme=$(snatcher config get client.theme)
.EE
.SS "Raw RPC calls"
.EX
# read the whole session, pick one field with jq
$ snatcher api session\-get \-\-jq '.arguments.version'

# call torrent-get with typed fields
$ snatcher api torrent\-get \-F ids:=1 \-F fields:='["id","name"]' \(rs
    \-\-jq '.arguments.torrents'
.EE
.SH "REPORTING BUGS"
Report bugs at https://github.com/itspriddle/snatcher/issues
